## Classification of Phishing Threats and Their Sources

Phishing attacks can be classified into different types based on their targets, delivery methods, and sources.

| Threat Type | Description | Main Source | Example |
|---|---|---|---|
| Email Phishing | Attackers send fake emails to steal information or make users click malicious links. | Cybercriminals, spam campaigns | Fake bank security email |
| Spear Phishing | A targeted phishing attack against a specific person or organization. | Cybercriminals, APT groups | Fake email sent to a company employee |
| Whaling | Phishing that targets senior employees such as CEOs or managers. | Cybercriminals, organized groups | Fake business request sent to a CEO |
| Smishing | Phishing messages delivered through SMS or text messages. | Cybercriminals, scammers | Fake delivery notification with a malicious link |
| Vishing | Attackers use phone or voice calls to trick victims into sharing sensitive information. | Scammers, cybercriminals | Fake call from a bank |
| Clone Phishing | Attackers copy a legitimate message but replace its link or attachment with a malicious one. | Cybercriminals | Copied company email with a fake login link |
| Credential Phishing | Fake login pages are used to steal usernames and passwords. | Cybercriminals | Fake Microsoft 365 login page |
| Malware Phishing | Phishing messages contain malicious files or links that can install malware. | Cybercriminals, malware operators | Email with a malicious attachment |
| Business Email Compromise (BEC) | Attackers impersonate a trusted business person to request money or sensitive information. | Fraud groups, cybercriminals | Fake payment request from a manager |
| Social Media Phishing | Attackers use social media messages or fake accounts to steal information. | Scammers, compromised accounts | Fake account recovery message |

### Main Sources of Phishing Threats

- Cybercriminals – attackers who use phishing to steal money, credentials, or personal information.
- Organized cybercrime groups – groups that perform large-scale phishing and fraud campaigns.
- APT groups – advanced threat actors that may use spear phishing for targeted attacks.
- Scammers – individuals or groups that use fake messages, calls, and websites to trick users.
- Compromised accounts – legitimate accounts that attackers take over and use for phishing.
- Botnets – networks of compromised devices that can distribute phishing and spam messages.

## Summary

Phishing threats use different methods such as email, SMS, phone calls, social media, and fake websites. Their sources can include scammers, cybercriminal groups, APT groups, compromised accounts, and botnets. Classifying these threats helps CTI analysts understand how phishing attacks are delivered and where they come from.
